      "page_title": "Mapping Foundations",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Definition",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "A ==mutable mapping== of hashable keys to arbitrary values. Implemented as a ==hash table==.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Key Constraints",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Type",
            "Hashable?",
            "Reason"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "int, str, tuple",
              "Yes",
              "Immutable"
            ],
            [
              "list, dict",
              "**No**",
              "Mutable"
            ],
            [
              "set",
              "**No**",
              "Mutable"
            ]
          ],
          "frame": "dashed"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Identity vs Equality",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "d = {1: 'a', True: 'b'}",
            "print(d)  # {1: 'b'}",
            "# 1 == True is True",
            "# hash(1) == hash(True)"
          ],
          "note": "True overwrites 1"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Ordering",
          "ink": "orange"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Insertion order guaranteed (3.7+)",
            "Not sorted by key",
            "Not lexicographic"
          ],
          "style": "dot",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "orange"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Common Mistakes",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Using list as key → TypeError",
            "Assuming order is sorted",
            "Thinking 1 and True are different"
          ],
          "style": "cross",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "6. Comparison",
          "ink": "black"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Feature",
            "Dict",
            "Set"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Key-Value",
              "Yes",
              "No"
            ],
            [
              "Duplicate Values",
              "Yes",
              "No"
            ],
            [
              "Ordered",
              "Yes",
              "No"
            ]
          ],
          "frame": "dashed"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Summary",
          "text": "Ordered, mutable mappings. Hashable keys required. 1 and True are indistinguishable."
        }
      ],
      "hero": "python",
      "subtitle": "Core Mechanics"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Creating and Reading",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Creation Methods",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Use ==literal syntax== for static data. It is the most efficient way to create small dictionaries.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "d1 = {'a': 1, 'b': 2}  # literal",
            "d2 = dict(a=1, b=2)    # kwargs",
            "d3 = dict([('a', 1)])   # pairs"
          ],
          "note": "kwargs keys must be identifiers"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Access Patterns",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Method",
            "Missing Key",
            "Side Effect"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "d[key]",
              "KeyError",
              "None"
            ],
            [
              "d.get(k, dflt)",
              "Returns dflt",
              "None"
            ],
            [
              "'k' in d",
              "False",
              "None"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 1
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "d = {'a': 1}",
            "print(d['b'])       # KeyError!",
            "print(d.get('b', 0)) # 0",
            "print('b' in d)      # False"
          ],
          "note": "get() does NOT insert key"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Membership Check",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`'key' in d` checks **keys only**",
            "Complexity: ==O(1)== average time",
            "Does not check values"
          ],
          "style": "dot",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Common Mistakes",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Using `d.get(k)` to check existence",
            "Ambiguous if `None` is a valid value",
            "Confusing `d[k]` (raises) vs `d.get(k)`"
          ],
          "style": "cross",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "sticky",
          "title": "⚠️ Gotcha",
          "text": "If `None` is a valid value, `d.get(k)` returning `None` is ambiguous. Use `k in d` for existence checks."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Summary",
          "ink": "black"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Use **literals** for creation",
            "Use `d[key]` for guaranteed access",
            "Use `d.get()` for safe access with defaults"
          ],
          "style": "check",
          "frame": "solid",
          "ink": "black"
        }
      ],
      "hero": "python",
      "subtitle": "Syntax & Access"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Updating & Removing",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Assignment Mechanics",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "`d[key] = value` inserts new or overwrites existing. Overwriting does **NOT** change insertion order.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "d = {'a': 1, 'b': 2}",
            "d['a'] = 99  # overwrite",
            "d['c'] = 3   # insert",
            "list(d) # ['a', 'b', 'c']"
          ],
          "note": "Order stays 'a', 'b', 'c'"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Delete + Reinsert",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "`del d[key]` then `d[key] = val` moves key to the **end** of insertion order.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "d = {'a': 1, 'b': 2}",
            "del d['a']",
            "d['a'] = 99",
            "list(d) # ['b', 'a']"
          ],
          "note": "'a' moves to end"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. pop() Behavior",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "`pop(key, default)` removes and returns value. Missing key returns `default` instead of raising `KeyError`.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "d = {'a': 1}",
            "val = d.pop('b', 'missing')",
            "val # 'missing'",
            "d # {'a': 1} (unchanged)"
          ],
          "note": "Safe removal with fallback"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. setdefault() Logic",
          "ink": "orange"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Returns value if key exists. If missing, inserts key with `default` and returns it.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "orange"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "d = {}",
            "d.setdefault('a', 1) # 1",
            "d # {'a': 1}",
            "d.setdefault('a', 99) # 1"
          ],
          "note": "Second call returns 1"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Comparison Table",
          "ink": "black"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Method",
            "Order Effect",
            "Return Value"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "d[k]=v",
              "Preserves (if exists)",
              "None"
            ],
            [
              "del + insert",
              "Moves to end",
              "None"
            ],
            [
              "pop(k, d)",
              "Removes key",
              "Value or default"
            ],
            [
              "setdefault(k, d)",
              "Inserts if missing",
              "Value or default"
            ]
          ],
          "frame": "solid"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "6. Common Mistakes",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Using `setdefault` with complex defaults that have side effects (evaluated even if key exists).",
            "Assuming `pop()` without default is safe; it raises `KeyError` if key missing."
          ],
          "style": "cross",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Summary",
          "text": "Assignment preserves order for existing keys; `pop` and `setdefault` provide safe removal and conditional insertion."
        }
      ],
      "hero": "python",
      "subtitle": "Order & Safety"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Iteration, Views & Order",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Iteration Basics",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Iterating `for key in d` yields keys in ==insertion order==. Use `d.items()` for pairs.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "d = {'a': 1, 'b': 2}",
            "for k in d: print(k)  # a, b",
            "for k, v in d.items():",
            "    print(k, v)  # a 1, b 2"
          ],
          "note": "Order is insertion, not sorted"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Dynamic Views",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "`d.keys()`, `d.values()`, `d.items()` return ==view objects==, not copies. They reflect live changes.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "d = {'a': 1}",
            "v = d.values()",
            "d['b'] = 2",
            "print(list(v))  # [1, 2]"
          ],
          "note": "View updates automatically"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. View Operations",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Views support set ops: `&`, `|`, `-`",
            "Membership: `x in d.keys()` is O(1)",
            "Find common keys: `d1.keys() & d2.keys()`"
          ],
          "style": "dot",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "View",
            "Set-like",
            "Indexable",
            "Use Case"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`d.keys()`",
              "Yes",
              "No",
              "Membership, Set Ops"
            ],
            [
              "`d.values()`",
              "No",
              "No",
              "Iteration only"
            ],
            [
              "`d.items()`",
              "No",
              "No",
              "Pair iteration"
            ]
          ],
          "frame": "solid"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Order Guarantee",
          "ink": "orange"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Iteration follows ==insertion order==, not sorted order. Do not rely on sorted output without explicit sorting.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "orange"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "d = {'z': 1, 'a': 2}",
            "print(list(d))  # ['z', 'a']",
            "print(sorted(d))  # ['a', 'z']"
          ],
          "note": "Use sorted() if needed"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Common Mistakes",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Modifying dict during iteration raises **RuntimeError**",
            "`d.values()[0]` fails: views are not lists",
            "Assuming `d.values()` is a set (it is not)"
          ],
          "style": "cross",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "d = {'a': 1, 'b': 2}",
            "for k in d:",
            "    d['c'] = 3  # RuntimeError!"
          ],
          "note": "Dict changed size during iteration"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "6. Performance",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Operation",
            "Complexity",
            "Note"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Iterate keys",
              "O(n)",
              "Full scan"
            ],
            [
              "`k in d.keys()`",
              "O(1)",
              "Hash lookup"
            ],
            [
              "`v in d.values()`",
              "O(n)",
              "Linear scan"
            ]
          ],
          "frame": "solid"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Key Takeaway",
          "text": "Views are dynamic and set-like for keys. Iteration is insertion order. Never modify dict during iteration."
        }
      ],
      "hero": "python",
      "subtitle": "Dynamic Views"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Dictionary Comprehensions",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. The Template",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "{key_expr: value_expr",
            " for item in iterable",
            " if condition}",
            "",
            "# Basic: squares 0-4",
            "{x: x**2 for x in range(5)}"
          ],
          "note": "Creates {0:0, 1:1, 2:4, 3:9, 4:16}"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "The ==syntax== mirrors list comprehensions but outputs a mapping. It iterates once, building the dict in a single pass.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Filter & Transform",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "# Filter: keep v > 0",
            "{k: v for k, v in d.items()",
            " if v > 0}",
            "",
            "# Transform: uppercase keys",
            "{k.upper(): v for k, v in d.items()}"
          ],
          "note": "Filtering + Transform in one step"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "**Filtering**: `if` clause drops unwanted items.",
            "**Transforming**: Modify keys or values on the fly.",
            "Replaces verbose `for` loops with one line."
          ],
          "style": "dot",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Performance",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Method",
            "Speed",
            "Readability"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Comprehension",
              "Fastest",
              "High"
            ],
            [
              "Loop + Assign",
              "Slower",
              "Medium"
            ],
            [
              "dict(zip())",
              "Fast",
              "Low (no filter)"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 0,
          "frame": "dashed"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Comprehensions use ==optimized bytecode==, avoiding the overhead of repeated `d[key] = value` lookups in a loop.",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Nested Dicts",
          "ink": "black"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "{k: {k2: v2 for k2, v2 in v.items()}",
            " for k, v in d.items()}",
            "",
            "# Creates nested structure",
            "# {'a': {'x': 1}, 'b': {'y': 2}}"
          ],
          "note": "Outer loop iterates top-level keys"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Use for **grouping** or **hierarchical** data.",
            "Read right-to-left: inner comp first."
          ],
          "style": "dot"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Common Mistakes",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "**Duplicate Keys**: Last occurrence wins (unintended).",
            "**Over-complexity**: Don't nest >2 levels.",
            "**Side Effects**: Avoid `append` inside comp."
          ],
          "style": "cross",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "sticky",
          "title": "Gotcha",
          "text": "If keys collide in the source iterable, the **last** value overwrites previous ones silently."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "6. Summary",
          "ink": "black"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Preferred for creating dicts from iterables.",
            "Faster than loops, more concise than `dict()`.",
            "Supports filtering and transformation inline."
          ],
          "style": "check"
        }
      ],
      "hero": "python",
      "subtitle": "Fast & Concise Creation"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Hashable Keys & Performance",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Hashability Contract",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Keys must implement `__hash__` and `__eq__`. In practice, hashable objects are ==immutable== (e.g., `str`, `int`, `tuple`).",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "hash('hello')  # stable int",
            "hash((1, 2))   # tuple OK",
            "# hash([1, 2]) # TypeError!"
          ],
          "note": "Lists are unhashable"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Hash Table Mechanics",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Dicts use a **hash table** structure.",
            "Lookup is average ==O(1)==, worst-case O(n).",
            "Worst case occurs due to **collisions**.",
            "Python uses open addressing + probe sequence."
          ],
          "style": "dot",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              "Key",
              "hash(key)",
              "Slot Index",
              "Probe if Busy",
              "Value"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Complexity Comparison",
          "ink": "black"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Operation",
            "Dict",
            "List",
            "Set"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Lookup",
              "O(1) avg",
              "O(n)",
              "O(1) avg"
            ],
            [
              "Memory",
              "High (K+V)",
              "Low",
              "Med (K only)"
            ],
            [
              "Order",
              "Insertion",
              "Index",
              "None"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 0
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Dict lookup is faster than list search (O(1) vs O(n)). Sets are similar speed but store no values.",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Memory & Key Choice",
          "ink": "orange"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Dicts use more memory than sets/lists.",
            "Overhead from hash table + key-value pairs.",
            "Avoid large, complex objects as keys.",
            "Hashing big objects is slow & memory-heavy."
          ],
          "style": "dot",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "orange"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "# BAD: List as key (unhashable)",
            "# d = {[1,2]: 'val'}",
            "",
            "# GOOD: Tuple as key (hashable)",
            "d = {(1, 2): 'val'}"
          ],
          "note": "Use tuples for composite keys"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Common Mistakes",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Using unhashable types (list, dict) as keys.",
            "Assuming O(1) is **guaranteed** (it's not).",
            "Adversarial inputs can cause O(n) collisions.",
            "Using expensive-to-hash objects as keys."
          ],
          "style": "cross",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "sticky",
          "title": "Optimization Tip",
          "text": "Use `frozenset` or `tuple` for composite keys. `frozenset` is hashable; `list` is not."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "6. Summary",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Hashable keys enable O(1) average lookup.",
            "Performance depends on hash quality.",
            "Collision rate determines worst-case speed.",
            "Choose simple, immutable keys for best perf."
          ],
          "style": "check",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "purple"
        }
      ],
      "hero": "python",
      "subtitle": "Mechanics & Speed"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Nested Dicts & Copying",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Nested Structure",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Values can be other dicts. Creates a nested structure.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "d = {'a': {'x': 1},",
            "     'b': {'y': 2}}",
            "# nested structure"
          ],
          "note": "values are dicts"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Shallow Copy",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`d.copy()` or `dict(d)` creates new dict",
            "Keys/values copied, nested objects **shared**",
            "Faster: O(k) where k = top-level keys"
          ],
          "style": "dot",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "d = {'a': [1, 2]}",
            "c = d.copy()",
            "c['a'].append(3)",
            "print(d['a'])  # [1, 2, 3]"
          ],
          "note": "list is shared!"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Deep Copy",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Use `copy.deepcopy(d)` to recursively copy nested objects.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "import copy",
            "d = {'a': [1, 2]}",
            "c = copy.deepcopy(d)",
            "c['a'].append(3)",
            "print(d['a'])  # [1, 2]"
          ],
          "note": "fully independent"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Comparison",
          "ink": "black"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Feature",
            "Shallow",
            "Deep"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Method",
              "`d.copy()`",
              "`deepcopy(d)`"
            ],
            [
              "Nested",
              "Shared",
              "Copied"
            ],
            [
              "Speed",
              "O(k) fast",
              "O(n) slow"
            ],
            [
              "Use",
              "Flat dicts",
              "Nested"
            ]
          ],
          "frame": "solid"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Common Mistakes",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Assuming `d.copy()` is deep copy",
            "Modifying nested list in copied dict",
            "Expecting original to be unchanged"
          ],
          "style": "cross",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "sticky",
          "title": "Key Rule",
          "text": "Shallow = fast but shared. Deep = slow but independent."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "6. Summary",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Use shallow for flat dicts",
            "Use deep for nested structures",
            "Be aware of shared references"
          ],
          "style": "check",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        }
      ],
      "hero": "python",
      "subtitle": "Shallow vs Deep"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Counting and Grouping",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Counter Basics",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "A `dict` subclass for counting hashable elements. `Counter(iterable)` counts occurrences automatically.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "from collections import Counter",
            "c = Counter('abracadabra')",
            "print(c)",
            "# {'a': 5, 'b': 2, 'r': 2, 'c': 1, 'd': 1}"
          ],
          "note": "counts chars in string"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. most_common(n)",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Returns top `n` elements as `(element, count)` tuples. Useful for frequency analysis.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "c = Counter('abracadabra')",
            "top2 = c.most_common(2)",
            "print(top2)",
            "# [('a', 5), ('b', 2)]"
          ],
          "note": "sorted by count desc"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. defaultdict Basics",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "A `dict` subclass that calls a factory for missing keys. `defaultdict(list)` creates empty lists.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "from collections import defaultdict",
            "d = defaultdict(list)",
            "d['a'].append(1)",
            "d['b'].append(2)",
            "print(d['c'])  # [] (auto-created)"
          ],
          "note": "no KeyError on access"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Grouping Pattern",
          "ink": "black"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Use `defaultdict(list)` to group items by key. More concise than manual key checking.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "black"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "groups = defaultdict(list)",
            "for item in items:",
            "    key = get_key(item)",
            "    groups[key].append(item)"
          ],
          "note": "auto-creates lists"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Common Mistake",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "❌ Checking `if key in d` before appending",
            "❌ Using regular `dict` for grouping",
            "✅ `defaultdict` handles missing keys automatically"
          ],
          "style": "cross",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "6. Comparison Table",
          "ink": "black"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Feature",
            "Counter",
            "defaultdict"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Purpose",
              "Counting",
              "Grouping"
            ],
            [
              "Factory",
              "None (counts)",
              "Any callable"
            ],
            [
              "Access",
              "Returns 0 if missing",
              "Creates default"
            ],
            [
              "Use Case",
              "Frequency",
              "Aggregation"
            ]
          ],
          "frame": "solid"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "7. Performance",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Access: O(1) average for both",
            "`Counter.most_common(n)`: O(n log n)",
            "Sorting dominates for large n"
          ],
          "style": "dot",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "8. Summary",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "✅ Use `Counter` for counting",
            "✅ Use `defaultdict` for grouping",
            "✅ Both simplify common patterns"
          ],
          "style": "check",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        }
      ],
      "hero": "python",
      "subtitle": "Counter & defaultdict"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Merging Data & Config",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Union Operator (|)",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "==d1 | d2== creates a **new** dict. If keys overlap, d2's values win (last value wins).",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "d1 = {'a': 1, 'b': 2}",
            "d2 = {'b': 3, 'c': 4}",
            "res = d1 | d2",
            "# res: {'a': 1, 'b': 3, 'c': 4}"
          ],
          "note": "d1 remains unchanged"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. In-Place Union (|=)",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "==d1 |= d2== updates d1 with d2's keys. d2 overwrites d1 for common keys.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "d1 = {'a': 1, 'b': 2}",
            "d2 = {'b': 3, 'c': 4}",
            "d1 |= d2",
            "# d1: {'a': 1, 'b': 3, 'c': 4}"
          ],
          "note": "d1 is modified directly"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. update() Method",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "==d1.update(d2)== updates d1 in place. Works in all Python versions.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "d1 = {'a': 1, 'b': 2}",
            "d2 = {'b': 3, 'c': 4}",
            "d1.update(d2)",
            "# d1: {'a': 1, 'b': 3, 'c': 4}"
          ],
          "note": "compatible with Python < 3.9"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Comparison Table",
          "ink": "black"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Operation",
            "Result",
            "In-Place?",
            "Version"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "d1 | d2",
              "New dict",
              "No",
              "3.9+"
            ],
            [
              "d1 |= d2",
              "Updates d1",
              "Yes",
              "3.9+"
            ],
            [
              "d1.update(d2)",
              "Updates d1",
              "Yes",
              "All"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 0,
          "frame": "solid"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Config Merging",
          "ink": "orange"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Use ==|== to merge defaults with user config. User values should win.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "orange"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "defaults = {'theme': 'dark'}",
            "user = {'theme': 'light'}",
            "final = user | defaults",
            "# final: {'theme': 'light'}"
          ],
          "note": "user config overrides defaults"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "6. Common Mistakes",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Expecting d1 | d2 to modify d1 (it creates new dict)",
            "Using d1.update(d2) and expecting a new dict (it modifies d1)",
            "Assuming d1 |= d2 returns a new dict (it updates d1)"
          ],
          "style": "cross",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "7. Summary",
          "ink": "black"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Use ==|== for non-destructive merging (new dict)",
            "Use ==|=== or ==update()== for in-place merging",
            "Last value wins for overlapping keys in all cases"
          ],
          "style": "check",
          "frame": "solid",
          "ink": "black"
        }
      ],
      "hero": "python",
      "subtitle": "Union Operators"
    },
